===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=noun|:inh|enm:guide<ety:bor<fro:guide<ety:bor<pro:guida<ety:from<guidar<ety:bor<frk:*wītan>>>>>>>>|tree=1}}
c. 1325–75. From {{inh|en|enm|guide}}, from the {{der|en|fro|guide}}, from {{der|en|pro|guida}}, from {{m|pro|guidar}}, from {{der|en|frk|*wītan|t=to show the way, lead}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*wītaną|t=to see, know; go, depart}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*weyd-|t=to see, know}}. Cognate with {{cog|ang|wītan|t=to see, take heed to, watch after, guard, keep}}. Related also to {{cog|en|wit}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# Someone who [[#Verb|guides]], especially someone hired to show people around a [[place]] or an [[institution]] and offer [[information]] and [[explanation]], or to lead them through dangerous [[terrain]].
#: {{synonyms|en|guider|xenagogue<q:obsolete, rare>}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''guide''' led us around the museum and explained the exhibits.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Psalms|xlviii|14|text=He will be our '''guide''', even unto death.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1923|title=From a Terrace in Prague|author=w:Bernard Granville Baker|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/22776/pg22776-images.html|passage=Some tourists aggravate their position by hiring a '''guide'''. Every city of any historic importance breeds a class of mortals that are born '''guides'''; they have come to belong to the "staffage" of picturesque surroundings; and in this respect Prague is happily yet unspoilt.}}
# {{senseid|en|guidebook}} A [[document]] or [[book]] that offers information or [[instruction]]; [[guidebook]].
# {{senseid|en|sign}} A [[sign]] that guides people; [[guidepost]].
# Any marking or object that catches the eye to provide quick [[reference]].
# {{synonym of|en|legend}}, a [[key]] to [[symbol]]s, [[abbreviation]]s, and [[term]]s on a [[map]], [[chart]], [[etc.]]
# A [[device]] that guides part of a [[machine]], or guides [[motion]] or [[action]].
## A blade or channel for directing the flow of water to the buckets in a [[water wheel]].
## A [[grooved]] [[director]] for a [[probe]] or [[knife]] in [[surgery]].
## {{lb|en|printing|dated}} A strip or device to direct the [[compositor]]'s eye to the line of copy being set.
# {{lb|en|occult}} A [[spirit]] believed to speak through a [[medium]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=Rollo Ahmed|title=The Black Art|publisher=Long|location=London|page=75|passage=The familiars of the magicians, on the other hand, were not in all cases evil, and often may have approximated the "'''guides'''" with whom present-day spiritualists are well acquainted.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} A member of a group [[march]]ing in [[formation]] who sets the [[pattern]] of [[movement]] or [[alignment]] for the rest.

